= Solution
{parent=1}

The <complex modulus> is a nonnegative <real number>. Equality therefore requires $z-1$ itself to be real and nonnegative. Its <imaginary part> gives $y=0$, and its <real part> then gives $x-1\geq0$. Conversely every such point satisfies the equality. Hence the complete <solution set> is
$$
\boxed{y=0,\qquad x\geq1}.
$$
Geometrically this is the closed ray on the <real axis> starting at $z=1$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The common ratio in the two <plane> equations can be denoted by $u$. Their intersection then has $x=1+4u$, $y=2+3u$, and $z=-1+2u$. The direction vector $(4,3,2)$ has <Euclidean norm> $\sqrt{29}$, so the requested <unit vector> form is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=(1,2,-1)+\lambda\frac{(4,3,2)}{\sqrt{29}},\qquad\lambda\in\mathbb R}.
$$
Substituting this expression into both equations gives the same ratio $\lambda/\sqrt{29}$, verifying the entire line.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Apply the <multivariable chain rule>, keeping the other new coordinate fixed in each differentiation. At fixed $\theta$, the <derivatives> of $x,y$ with respect to $r$ are $\cos2\theta,\sin2\theta$. At fixed $r$, their <derivatives> with respect to $\theta$ are $-2r\sin2\theta,2r\cos2\theta$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial r}\right)_\theta
=\cos2\theta\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_y
+\sin2\theta\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_x},
$$
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial\theta}\right)_r
=-2r\sin2\theta\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_y
+2r\cos2\theta\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_x}.
$$
The factor two arises because the ordinary polar angle is $2\theta$.

