= Solution
{parent=a}

A double six has <probability> $p=1/36$ on each independent throw. For Alice to win on her second turn, her first throw and Bob's first throw must both fail, then her second throw must succeed. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P=\left(\frac{35}{36}\right)^2\frac1{36}=\frac{1225}{46656}}.
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $q=35/36$. Alice wins on her $(n+1)$st turn if $n$ complete rounds fail and then she succeeds. These disjoint events give the <geometric series>
$$
P_A=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}q^{2n}p=\frac{p}{1-q^2}
=\frac{1}{1+q}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{P_A=\frac{36}{71}}.
$$
The <probability> of never obtaining a winner is $\lim_{n\to\infty}q^{2n}=0$. The small advantage over $1/2$ comes from taking the first turn.

