= Solution
{parent=a}

**Yes.** The matrix is a real <symmetric matrix>, so the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> provides an <orthonormal basis> of real <eigenvectors> and hence a real diagonal form. Explicitly its <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda^2-7\lambda-4$, with distinct real <eigenvalues>
$$
\boxed{\lambda_\pm=\frac{7\pm\sqrt{65}}2}.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda^2-2\lambda\cos\theta+1$, with <eigenvalues> $\cos\theta\pm i\sin\theta$. A real diagonal form can have only real <eigenvalues>, so it requires $\sin\theta=0$. Conversely, when $\theta=k\pi$, the <rotation matrix> is already $(-1)^kI$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{real diagonalization is possible exactly when }\theta\in\pi\mathbb Z}.
$$

