The independent vectors $\mathbf a,\mathbf b$ span the <plane> through the origin, and their <cross product> is a nonzero <normal vector>. Thus the required nonparametric equation is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)=0}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The orientation assumption gives $\mathbf c\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)>0$. The same open <half-space> is therefore characterized by
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)>0}.
$$
Equality would put the point in the separating <plane>, rather than strictly on the same side.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take two edge vectors in the face, $\mathbf b-\mathbf a$ and $\mathbf c-\mathbf a$. Their <cross product> is the face <normal vector>
$$
\mathbf n=(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)\times(\mathbf c-\mathbf a)
=\mathbf a\times\mathbf b+\mathbf b\times\mathbf c+\mathbf c\times\mathbf a.
$$
The face <plane> is $(\mathbf r-\mathbf a)\cdot\mathbf n=0$. Every term in $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf n$ except $\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)$ vanishes, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b+\mathbf b\times\mathbf c+\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)
=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)}.
$$
The <scalar triple product> is nonzero, so this normal cannot vanish.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $D=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)>0$. Since these vectors form a <basis>, write $\mathbf r=\alpha\mathbf a+\beta\mathbf b+\gamma\mathbf c$. The <reciprocal basis> gives
$$
\alpha=\frac{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)}D,\qquad
\beta=\frac{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)}D,\qquad
\gamma=\frac{\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)}D.
$$
A point is in the closed <tetrahedron> precisely when it is a <convex combination> of the four vertices, with weights $1-\alpha-\beta-\gamma,\alpha,\beta,\gamma$. Thus the necessary and sufficient conditions are
$$
\boxed{\begin{gathered}
\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\geq0,\quad
\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)\geq0,\quad
\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\geq0,\\
\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b+\mathbf b\times\mathbf c+\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)\leq D.
\end{gathered}}
$$
These four <half-spaces> are the four inward sides of its faces. This is <tetrahedron membership via scalar triple products>; making all inequalities strict describes the interior.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The opposite face lies in the <plane> spanned by $\mathbf a,\mathbf b$ through the origin. Directly,
$$
\mathbf a\times\mathbf b=(-2,2,4),\qquad
\mathbf c\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)=12,\qquad
|\mathbf a\times\mathbf b|=2\sqrt6.
$$
The <distance from a point to a plane> is the absolute normal component, so
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{12}{2\sqrt6}=\sqrt6}.
$$
Here $\mathbf c=(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)/2$, so the perpendicular foot is the origin, a vertex of the opposite triangular face. Thus the distance to the actual face equals the distance to its containing <plane>.

