= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $R=|b|$ so that the radial range respects the stated allowance $b\ne0$. With the azimuth measured from the starting meridian, the <cylindrical coordinates> of the curved <surface> satisfy
$$
\boxed{0\leq r\leq R,\qquad0\leq\theta\leq\alpha,\qquad
z=a\left(1-\frac{r^2}{b^2}\right)}.
$$
At $r=0$ all azimuths describe the same apex. The <surface> is a sector of an <elliptic paraboloid>.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <volume element> in <cylindrical coordinates> is $r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta$. Integrating from the base to the curved <surface> gives
$$
V=\int_0^\alpha\!d\theta\int_0^R\!r\,dr\int_0^{a(1-r^2/b^2)}\!dz
=\alpha a\left[\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{r^4}{4b^2}\right]_0^R.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{V=\frac{\alpha ab^2}{4}}.
$$
For a complete rotation this is half the <volume> of the enclosing cylinder.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <embedded surface parametrization>
$$
\mathbf X(r,\theta)=\left(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta,a\left(1-\frac{r^2}{b^2}\right)\right).
$$
Its tangent vectors have <cross product>
$$
\mathbf X_r\times\mathbf X_\theta
=\left(\frac{2ar^2}{b^2}\cos\theta,
\frac{2ar^2}{b^2}\sin\theta,r\right),
$$
whose magnitude is $r\sqrt{1+4a^2r^2/b^4}$. Thus the <surface area> is
$$
A=\alpha\int_0^R r\sqrt{1+\frac{4a^2r^2}{b^4}}\,dr.
$$
With $u=1+4a^2r^2/b^4$, the radial integral becomes $b^4(8a^2)^{-1}\int u^{1/2}\,du$. Evaluating its endpoints gives
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{\alpha b^4}{12a^2}
\left[\left(1+\frac{4a^2}{b^2}\right)^{3/2}-1\right]}.
$$
This is the scalar-area part of the <paraboloid sector surface measures>.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $a/|b|\ll1$, apply the <binomial series> to the result in (c):
$$
\left(1+\frac{4a^2}{b^2}\right)^{3/2}
=1+\frac{6a^2}{b^2}+\frac{6a^4}{b^4}+O(a^6/b^6).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{\alpha b^2}{2}+\frac{\alpha a^2}{2}+O(a^4/b^2)
\sim\frac{\alpha b^2}{2}}.
$$
For $\alpha=2\pi$, the leading term is $\pi b^2$, the <area> of the base disk. A very shallow cap is nearly planar; its first <area> excess is $\pi a^2$.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The upward orientation of $\mathbf X_r\times\mathbf X_\theta$ corresponds to traversing the boundary from the apex to the starting rim, around the rim in increasing azimuth, and back to the apex. This is the indicated $ABCA$ orientation and is outward from the solid. Integrate that <vector area element> directly:
$$
\mathbf S=\int_0^\alpha\!\int_0^R
\left(\frac{2ar^2}{b^2}\cos\theta,
\frac{2ar^2}{b^2}\sin\theta,r\right)\,dr\,d\theta.
$$
The radial integrals are $2aR/3$ in the horizontal components and $b^2/2$ in the vertical component. Hence the <vector area> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S_{ABCA}
=\frac{2a|b|}{3}\sin\alpha\,\mathbf i
+\frac{2a|b|}{3}(1-\cos\alpha)\,\mathbf j
+\frac{\alpha b^2}{2}\,\mathbf k}.
$$
The absolute value is needed only because the original statement permits negative $b$. Reversing the boundary orientation reverses the entire vector. For a full rotation the horizontal components cancel, leaving $\pi b^2\mathbf k$, the signed projected <area> of the base.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
