= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The homogeneous <linear system> has <solution set> $\ker A$, a <vector subspace>. If it contains only the zero vector, no two different solutions of the full system can exist: their difference would be a nonzero homogeneous solution.

A <particular solution> $\mathbf v_p$ to the full system exists exactly when $\mathbf w$ belongs to the <image of a linear map> $\operatorname{im}A$. If it exists, every solution is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v=\mathbf v_p+\mathbf v_h,\qquad\mathbf v_h\in\ker A}.
$$
Indeed, subtracting $A\mathbf v_p=\mathbf w$ from $A\mathbf v=\mathbf w$ gives $A(\mathbf v-\mathbf v_p)=0$, and conversely adding a <kernel> vector preserves the right-hand side. Thus there are **no solutions**, **one solution** if the <kernel> is trivial, or **infinitely many solutions** if it is nontrivial. The last <solution set> is an <affine subspace> of <dimension> $\dim\ker A$, determined by the <rank-nullity theorem>. For a square nonsingular matrix there is one solution for every right-hand side.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Subtracting the third row from the first gives $z=-1$, and the third row gives $y=2-x$. The second row then reduces to
$$
(1-t)x=s-t.
$$
For $t\ne1$, the unique solution is
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{s-t}{1-t},\qquad y=\frac{2-t-s}{1-t},\qquad z=-1}.
$$
Consequently $s=1$ gives $\boxed{(x,y,z)=(1,1,-1)}$ for every $t\ne1$, whereas $s=6$ gives
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\frac{6-t}{1-t},\frac{-4-t}{1-t},-1\right),\qquad t\ne1}.
$$

When $t=1$, the first and second left-hand sides coincide. If $s=1$, they are the same <plane>, and the remaining independent <plane> cuts it in the line
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(\lambda,2-\lambda,-1),\qquad\lambda\in\mathbb R}.
$$
If $s=6$, the two <planes> are distinct and parallel, so **there is no solution**. Geometrically, for $t\ne1$ the three <planes> intersect at a single point; at $t=1$ the coincident-plane case leaves an entire line, while the parallel-plane case has empty intersection.

