Let $X$ count green balls. Sampling without replacement gives a <hypergeometric distribution>. The possible values are $0,1,2$, and counting equally likely three-element samples gives
$$
P(X=k)=\frac{\binom2k\binom3{3-k}}{\binom53}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(X=0)=\frac1{10},\qquad P(X=1)=\frac35,\qquad P(X=2)=\frac3{10}}.
$$
Their sum is $(1+6+3)/10=1$, as required for a <probability distribution>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Compute the first two moments from the <probability distribution>:
$$
\mu=E[X]=\frac6{10}+\frac6{10}=\frac65,\qquad
E[X^2]=\frac6{10}+\frac{12}{10}=\frac95.
$$
The <variance> is $E[X^2]-E[X]^2=9/5-36/25=9/25$, so the <expected value> and <standard deviation> are
$$
\boxed{\mu=\frac65,\qquad\sigma=\frac35}.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The interval within one <standard deviation> is $[3/5,9/5]$. Of the possible integer outcomes $0,1,2$, only $1$ lies in this interval. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(|X-\mu|\leq\sigma)=P(X=1)=\frac35}.
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Three draws cannot all be green because there are only two green balls. Thus **the <probability> of at least one red ball is $1$**. At least two red balls means $X\leq1$, so
$$
\boxed{P(\text{at least two red})=\frac1{10}+\frac35=\frac7{10}}.
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $Y$ indicate whether the draw from the second bag is green. It is independent of $X$, with $P(Y=1)=1/3$ and $P(Y=0)=2/3$. Exactly one green overall arises from the disjoint alternatives $(X,Y)=(1,0)$ or $(0,1)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(X+Y=1)=\frac35\frac23+\frac1{10}\frac13=\frac{13}{30}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The sole green ball comes from the first bag exactly on the event $(X,Y)=(1,0)$. Divide this joint <probability> by the conditioning event's <probability>:
$$
\boxed{P(X=1,Y=0\mid X+Y=1)
=\frac{(3/5)(2/3)}{13/30}=\frac{12}{13}}.
$$
This is the <conditional probability> form of <Bayes' theorem>.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Taking the second <partial derivatives> of the <polynomial> gives
$$
w_{xx}=\frac{180x^2y^2-30x^4+30y^4}{360},\qquad
w_{yy}=\frac{180x^2y^2+30x^4-30y^4}{360}.
$$
Adding cancels the pure fourth powers and leaves
$$
\boxed{w_{xx}+w_{yy}=x^2y^2}.
$$
Thus direct substitution verifies the stated <Poisson equation>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**Yes.** The <differential operator> $\mathcal L=2y\partial_x-x\partial_y$ is linear, even though its coefficients depend on position. For constants $A,B$,
$$
\mathcal L(Au_1+Bu_2)=A\mathcal Lu_1+B\mathcal Lu_2.
$$
If each homogeneous right-hand side is zero, so is their combination. Hence the <superposition principle> holds.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The homogeneous equation is the <dot product> condition $(2y,-x)\cdot\nabla u=0$. A suitable <vector field> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v(x,y)=(2y,-x)}.
$$
Where nonzero, it is tangent to the level curves of $u$ and perpendicular to its <gradient>. At the origin this vector vanishes, so no direction is defined there.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $\phi=x^2+2y^2$, the <gradient> is $\nabla\phi=(2x,4y)$, and
$$
(2y,-x)\cdot(2x,4y)=0.
$$
Away from the origin, both $\nabla\phi$ and $\nabla u$ lie in the one-dimensional subspace perpendicular to $(2y,-x)$, so they are parallel. More precisely,
$$
\boxed{\nabla u=\lambda(x,y)\nabla\phi}
$$
for some scalar $\lambda$, including $\lambda=0$. If $\nabla u\ne0$, this can also be inverted to express $\nabla\phi$ as a multiple of $\nabla u$. At a <stationary point> of $u$ with $\nabla\phi\ne0$, that literal inverse wording is not valid; the geometric assertion is parallelism of the nonzero <gradient> directions.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Write $u(x,y)=F(\phi(x,y))$ for any differentiable $F$. The <multivariable chain rule> gives $u_x=2xF'(\phi)$ and $u_y=4yF'(\phi)$. Consequently
$$
2yu_x-xu_y=4xyF'(\phi)-4xyF'(\phi)=0.
$$
Thus $\boxed{u=F(x^2+2y^2)}$ solves the homogeneous equation. The <method of characteristics> also explains completeness on the full <plane>: the <characteristic curves> are the connected <ellipses> $x^2+2y^2=\text{constant}$, and $u$ is constant on each one. These are the <elliptic characteristics of a planar transport equation>.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For a <monomial> trial $u_p=Ax^my^n$, the operator gives
$$
\mathcal Lu_p=2Amx^{m-1}y^{n+1}-Anx^{m+1}y^{n-1}.
$$
Choosing $m=n=2$ matches the two powers in the forcing, and $A=1/2$ matches their coefficients. A <particular solution> is therefore $u_p=x^2y^2/2$. Adding the homogeneous solutions gives
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=\frac12x^2y^2+F(x^2+2y^2)}.
$$
The arbitrary <function> labels the values on the <characteristic curves>; it must have sufficient regularity for the desired classical solution.

= vi
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Substitution of $y=1$ into the <general solution> gives
$$
\frac{x^2}{2}+F(x^2+2)=x^2.
$$
Thus $F(s)=(s-2)/2$ for $s\geq2$. Choosing this same formula everywhere gives the <polynomial> solution
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=\frac12x^2y^2+\frac12x^2+y^2-1}.
$$
It directly satisfies the differential equation and $u(x,1)=x^2$.

**The <boundary condition> does not determine a unique solution on the entire <plane>.** Every characteristic <ellipse> $x^2+2y^2<2$ misses the data line $y=1$. More generally, $F$ is arbitrary for $0\leq s<2$, subject to matching the chosen regularity at $s=2$ and at the origin. The displayed <polynomial> is one global extension; it is forced on $x^2+2y^2\geq2$. A smooth <function> supported strictly inside $0<s<2$ may be added to $F$ without changing the boundary data.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The required property is **odd symmetry**, $\boxed{f(-x)=-f(x)}$, up to irrelevant changes on a set of measure zero when discussing <Fourier coefficients>. An <odd function> has zero mean, and its products with <cosine> are odd, so every <cosine> coefficient vanishes. Its <sine> coefficients may remain nonzero.

For pointwise representation, the <Fourier series> takes the average of the one-sided limits at a jump. In particular a pure <Fourier sine series> has value zero at the origin and at the endpoints; arbitrary assigned values there need not equal the series sum.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the periodic extension of $f$ when the translated argument falls outside the original interval. With $k_n=2\pi n/L$, the <sine> subtraction identity gives
$$
\boxed{g(x)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty b_n
\left[\sin(k_nx)\cos(k_n\ell)-\cos(k_nx)\sin(k_n\ell)\right]}.
$$
This <translation of a Fourier series> generally introduces <cosine> terms: a translated <odd function> need not be odd about the original origin. Equality is understood wherever the original <Fourier series> represents its periodic extension, with midpoint values at jumps.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $\ell=mL$ with integer $m>0$, then $k_n\ell=2\pi nm$. All <sine> phase factors vanish and all <cosine> phase factors equal one. Therefore
$$
\boxed{g(x)=f(x)}
$$
for the periodic extension, and every <Fourier coefficient> is unchanged.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write the translated <Fourier series> in the convention $a_0^{(g)}/2+\sum_{n\geq1}(a_n^{(g)}\cos k_nx+b_n^{(g)}\sin k_nx)$. Comparing with (i) gives
$$
\boxed{a_0^{(g)}=0,\qquad a_n^{(g)}=-b_n\sin\frac{2\pi n\ell}{L},\qquad
b_n^{(g)}=b_n\cos\frac{2\pi n\ell}{L}}.
$$
The minus sign in the <cosine> coefficient records translation in the positive $x$ direction.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Apart from its endpoint convention, the ramp is odd, so its constant and <cosine> <Fourier coefficients> vanish. For the <sine> coefficient, evenness of the integrand and <integration by parts> give
$$
b_n=\frac{4K}{L^2}\int_0^{L/2}x\sin\frac{2\pi nx}{L}\,dx
=\frac{4K}{L^2}\left[-\frac{xL}{2\pi n}\cos\frac{2\pi nx}{L}
+\frac{L^2}{4\pi^2n^2}\sin\frac{2\pi nx}{L}\right]_0^{L/2}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{a_0=a_n=0,\qquad b_n=\frac{K(-1)^{n+1}}{\pi n}}.
$$
This is the <Fourier series of a periodic sawtooth>. At the periodic jump, the series sums to zero rather than the assigned endpoint value $K/2$; changing a value at one endpoint does not change the coefficients.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Constant translation speed gives $\boxed{\ell(t)=vt}$, since the initial displacement is zero. Substituting into the <Fourier series> gives
$$
\boxed{g(x,t)=\frac K\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n
\sin\left[\frac{2\pi n}{L}(x-vt)\right]}.
$$
The periodic ramp moves in the positive $x$ direction for $v>0$. At the moving jumps, this series gives the average of the two one-sided values.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Not as ordinary convergent <Fourier series>**, for a nontrivial moving ramp. Formal spatial differentiation would give
$$
\frac{2K}{L}\sum_{n=1}^\infty(-1)^{n+1}
\cos\left[\frac{2\pi n}{L}(x-vt)\right],
$$
and formal time differentiation multiplies that expression by $-v$. The summands have nondecaying amplitude and do not tend to zero, so the ordinary series cannot converge. The periodic extension has jumps, violating the regularity needed for <termwise differentiation of a Fourier series>.

Away from the moving jumps, the classical <derivatives> exist and are $g_x=K/L$ and $g_t=-vK/L$. A <distributional derivative> also includes the <Dirac delta distribution> contributions at the jumps, so distributional differentiation is possible, but it is a different interpretation. If $v=0$, the time <derivative> is trivially zero; if $K=0$, both <derivatives> are zero.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let the fixed positive <volume> be $V=abc$. For an interior constrained extremum, <Lagrange multipliers> applied to the energy give
$$
-\frac{2A}{a^3}+\lambda bc=0,\qquad
-\frac{2A}{b^3}+\lambda ac=0,\qquad
-\frac{2A}{c^3}+\lambda ab=0.
$$
Multiplying these equations by $a,b,c$ shows that $2A/a^2=2A/b^2=2A/c^2=\lambda V$. Since the lengths are positive, $a=b=c=V^{1/3}$.

The <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> proves this is the <global minimum>:
$$
\frac1{a^2}+\frac1{b^2}+\frac1{c^2}\geq
3\left(\frac1{a^2b^2c^2}\right)^{1/3}=3V^{-2/3},
$$
with equality exactly for equal sides. Thus the optimal shape is a **cube**, and $\boxed{E_{\min}=3AV^{-2/3}}$.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $q=2.4048$ and let the fixed constraint value be $C>0$. For $V=\pi R^2H$, the <Lagrange multiplier> equations give
$$
2\pi RH=\lambda\frac{-2q^2}{R^3},\qquad
\pi R^2=\lambda\frac{-2\pi^2}{H^3}.
$$
Dividing removes $\lambda$ and yields
$$
\frac{2H}{R}=\frac{q^2H^3}{\pi^2R^3},\qquad
\boxed{\frac HR=\frac{\sqrt2\pi}{q}=\frac{\sqrt2\pi}{2.4048}}.
$$
To establish a minimum, rather than merely a stationary ratio, set $u=q^2/R^2$ and $v=\pi^2/H^2$. Then $u+v=C$ and $V=\pi^2q^2/(u\sqrt v)$. The denominator squared is $u^2(C-u)$; its <derivative> is $u(2C-3u)$, giving a unique maximum at $u=2C/3$, $v=C/3$. The <volume> tends to infinity at either endpoint. This proves the stated ratio is the unique <global minimum>, as in the <optimal cylinder for a reciprocal-square constraint>.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A trigonometric simplification avoids repeated differentiation:
$$
\frac{\tan x-\sin x}{\sin^3x}
=\frac{1-\cos x}{\cos x\sin^2x}
=\frac1{\cos x(1+\cos x)}
$$
for sufficiently small nonzero $x$. Continuity of <cosine> then gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\tan x-\sin x}{\sin^3x}=\frac12}.
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

This is the defining difference quotient for the <derivative> of <sine> at $a$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to a}\frac{\sin x-\sin a}{x-a}=\cos a}.
$$
The result holds for every real constant $a$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For every fixed real $a$, the base is positive once $x>|a|$. Taking its logarithm gives
$$
\log\left[\left(\frac{x+a}{x-a}\right)^x\right]
=x\left[\log\left(1+\frac ax\right)-\log\left(1-\frac ax\right)\right].
$$
The <Taylor series> of the logarithm makes the bracket $2a/x+O(x^{-3})$, so this logarithm tends to $2a$. Continuity of the exponential gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(\frac{x+a}{x-a}\right)^x=e^{2a}}.
$$
For $a=0$ the base is identically one, consistently giving the same answer.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Yes.** The <wave equation> is linear and homogeneous. For $\mathcal W=\partial_x^2-c^{-2}\partial_t^2$ and constants $A,B$,
$$
\mathcal W(Af_1+Bf_2)=A\mathcal Wf_1+B\mathcal Wf_2.
$$
If both terms on the right vanish, their combination is also a solution. Thus the <superposition principle> applies, and it also preserves homogeneous fixed-end <boundary conditions>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use <separation of variables>, $f=X(x)T(t)$, and set
$$
\frac{X''}{X}=\frac{T''}{c^2T}=\kappa.
$$
For $\kappa=-\lambda^2$, with $\lambda>0$, the separated solutions are
$$
\boxed{f=(A\cos\lambda x+B\sin\lambda x)
(C\cos c\lambda t+D\sin c\lambda t)}.
$$
For $\kappa=0$, they are
$$
\boxed{f=(Ax+B)(Ct+D)}.
$$
For $\kappa=+\lambda^2$, they are
$$
\boxed{f=(A\cosh\lambda x+B\sinh\lambda x)
(C\cosh c\lambda t+D\sinh c\lambda t)}.
$$
Arbitrary admissible superpositions of separated solutions are again solutions. An individual product is only a separated family, not the entire <general solution>. Without prescribed boundaries, the general twice-differentiable solution is equivalently <D'Alembert's formula>,
$$
\boxed{f(x,t)=F(x-ct)+G(x+ct)}.
$$
For example, changing to $\xi=x-ct$, $\eta=x+ct$ makes the differential equation $f_{\xi\eta}=0$, whose two integrations give this form.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At $x=0$ and $x=L$, the fixed-end <boundary conditions> require $X(0)=X(L)=0$. In the oscillatory family, the first condition removes the <cosine> term and the second requires $\sin\lambda L=0$, so $\lambda=n\pi/L$ with $n\geq1$. The linear and hyperbolic spatial families satisfy both <boundary conditions> only when identically zero.

Thus the nonzero <normal modes> and their <superposition> give
$$
\boxed{f(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty
\left[A_n\cos\frac{n\pi ct}{L}+B_n\sin\frac{n\pi ct}{L}\right]
\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}}.
$$
For initial displacement $h(x)$ and velocity $j(x)$ compatible with the fixed ends, <orthogonality> of the <sine> functions determines
$$
A_n=\frac2L\int_0^L h(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx,\qquad
B_n=\frac{2}{n\pi c}\int_0^L j(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx.
$$
These are all the string solutions in the usual class where the series has the regularity needed to satisfy the <wave equation>.

= d
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The initial displacement is already the first spatial <normal mode>, so <sine> <orthogonality> gives $A_1=1$ and every other $A_n=0$. Zero initial velocity gives every $B_n=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{f(x,t)=\sin\frac{\pi x}{L}\cos\frac{\pi ct}{L}}.
$$
Both fixed-end values vanish for all time, and differentiation shows that the initial velocity is zero. This is the fundamental <standing wave>, with <angular frequency> $\pi c/L$.
